// Write elision into one outcome of a branch whose thread joins again.
//
// The first thread is load buffering through x := r1, which sMRD may elide
// into the later x := 1, dropping the dependency of the store on r1 := y:
// x := 1 is then read by r2 := x and y := r2 feeds r1 := y, so r1 = 1. RC11
// forbids r1 = 1 (r1 := y po x := 1 rf r2 := x po y := r2 rf r1 := y is a
// po ∪ rf cycle).
//
// x := 1 lies in one outcome of a branch on r0 := w, inside the first thread
// of a parallel block, and z := 1 after the join is po-after both outcomes.
// MoRDor's fork rule for write elision asks that every event po-after the
// elided store, other than the eliding one, be po-comparable with it, and
// exempts events in conflict with it. The else branch's x := 2 is in conflict
// with x := 1 but shares the po-upper bound z := 1 with it.
//
// - Conflict exemption: x := 2 is exempt, x := r1 is elided into x := 1, and
//   r1 = 1 is allowed, as it is without the branch or without z := 1.
// - Strict exemption, conflict and no common po-upper bound: x := 2 is not
//   exempt, the elision is blocked, and r1 = 1 is forbidden. Dropping z := 1
//   allows it again.
//
// The strict exemption is the one this test asserts.
x := 0; y := 0; w := 0; z := 0;
{
  r1 := y;
  x := r1;
  r0 := w;
  if (r0 = 0) { x := 1 } else { x := 2 };
} ||| {
  r2 := x;
  y := r2;
} ||| {
  w := 1;
};
z := 1;
%%
forbid (r1 = 1) [sMRD]
forbid (r1 = 1) [RC11]
